 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-what-will-merge"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner D4 (2026-10-22, relayed by root): the agent's dynamic context carries a concise list of every change that will merge back to main, its branch's divergence from main, compact, every turn, in the per-turn part, not the cached prefix. The agent holds the main it forked from (`seon.agent/state` :base) and already diffs it every test run (`seon.program/changed-vars`, for the tests a change reaches). Correct: `seon.program/divergence` names the vars the branch added, redefined and removed and the schema keys it registered or changed (each db's :var/form and :schema/form datoms, as changed-vars read them; changed-vars becomes its vars); the turn observes it (`seon.turn/observe`) against the agent's `:base`, its branch's merge base with main (set at init, and by `seon.agent/merge-main!` after each merge: one place), into its basis, and `seon.context/current-state` shows it as `:will-merge` among the turn sections. Replaces nothing; the help page says what it is. Learned through the server (2026-10-22): against the main the agent forked from (`seon.agent/state` :base), a branch cut before main changed a function showed main's change as one the branch carries (`:changed [seon.context/found]` on a branch that never touched it); the merge base is what a merge compares against. A resumed branch's `:base` is now its merge base too, not the image of main's head. Proof: `seon.program-test/a-branch-that-merged-main-carries-only-its-own-changes`; the stale-branch case was shown through the server. Not covered: data entities a branch writes (`seon.merge/program`'s :datum keys) and multimethod and protocol definitions, which the full program merge sees (`seon.merge/program`, 440 ms per db, too slow for every turn)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/divergence-names-each-change-a-merge-carries
                      seon.context-test/the-turn-names-what-the-merge-will-carry}}
